This complete standalone JavaScript example uses a verified-session fixture, not an actual sign-in system:
```javascript
function update(session, entry, minutes) {
  if (!session) return { status: 401 };
  if (session.userId !== entry.ownerId) return { status: 403 };
  if (!Number.isInteger(minutes) || minutes < 1 || minutes > 180) {
    return { status: 400 };
  }
  entry.minutes = minutes;
  return { status: 200, entry };
}
const entry = { id: 9, ownerId: 'nora', minutes: 40 };
console.log(update(null, entry, 50));
console.log(update({ userId: 'luis' }, entry, 50));
console.log(entry.minutes);
```
The statuses are 401 and 403; minutes remains 40. 401 asks for valid sign-in, 403 denies this authenticated user's permission, and 400 rejects bad input.

Continue after the update helper with these separate record copies:
```javascript
const original = { id: 9, ownerId: 'nora', minutes: 40 };
const invalid = { ...original };
const allowed = { ...original };
console.log(update({ userId: 'nora' }, invalid, 0).status);
console.log(invalid.minutes);
console.log(update({ userId: 'nora' }, allowed, 50).status);
console.log(allowed.minutes);
console.log(original.minutes);
```
The outputs are 400, 40, 200, 50, 40. {...original} copies these flat fields, letting us verify a rejected update without changing the baseline. A successful assignment changes only the allowed copy.

An Express middleware function typically receives req, res, and next. It either sends an error response and returns, or calls next() to continue to the route. Validation middleware can reject a malformed body before writing. A final error handler can report a useful failure without exposing private records, passwords, or internal credentials. Responses and logs must not reveal another user's data just to explain permission denial.

A minimal continuation callback example is:
```javascript
function requireSession(req, res, next) {
  if (!req.session) return res.status(401).json({error: "Sign in"});
  next();
}
```
Express supplies next; calling it without an argument continues to later middleware. The missing-session branch returns before that call. This fragment requires an Express route and verified-session middleware; it is not a sign-in implementation.

Calling requireSession({session:{}}, {}, () => console.log("Continue")) prints Continue through the supplied callback. This local call demonstrates continuation only; the verified-session fixture is not authentication.

